//! lotus/stereo_crop.rye — ALES118: stereo_crop — Trim to Selection carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. ALES111 named `crop` — keep the `count` samples starting at `start`
//! and DROP every sample before and after — for a mono Clip. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips heard together whose defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold
//! the SAME length so the two channels stay aligned in time. This rung carries crop into stereo the way ALES117 carried cut: run ALES111's proven mono `crop` on each channel with the
//! SAME `start` and `count`, so the trim lands identically in both and the stereo image stays balanced. Where ALES117's stereo_cut REMOVES the named span and keeps everything around
//! it, stereo_crop KEEPS the named span and drops everything else — the exact stereo complement, so the pair teaches the one distinction about a selection on a master: REMOVE it, or
//! KEEP ONLY it.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: stereo_crop(sc, start, count) keeps [start, start+count) in both channels and drops the rest, reusing ALES111's mono `crop` per channel over its public API.
//! BOTH channels are validated BEFORE either is mutated — because crop can fault only with BadRange (a kept span outside the samples) and both channels enter equal-length, the shared
//! span is checked once against the common length up front, so once the check passes each mono crop is pre-validated to succeed and a refusal never desynchronises the channels. It
//! reuses ALES2's EditError whole and invents none of its own.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE STEREO CROP LAW: the left channel equals mono crop(left, start, count) and the right equals mono crop(right, start, count), each byte for byte. THE
//! BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both are exactly count — equal starting lengths cropped to the same span leave the channels aligned. THE COMPLEMENT
//! LAW: stereo_crop keeps exactly what ALES117's stereo_cut removes on each channel, the crop spliced back into the cut reconstructing the source per channel. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE
//! LAW: any refusal leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; crop(0, len) the identity on both, crop(start, 0) the empty pair, a span past the samples BadRange.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES118.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260815-015904_fill-ales118-lotus-stereo-crop.md
//! Stands on: lotus/crop.rye (ALES111) — the mono Trim to Selection run once per channel; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance invariant;
//! lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, its cut/splice used by the selftest to prove the complement law, and the EditError reused whole; lotus/meter.rye (ALES13) — the peak/RMS meter.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It reseats an existing span to the front of each channel and
//! shortens the length — it invents no sample, changes no value, and reads no byte past either channel's len. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no
//! real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_crop.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const crop = @import("crop.rye");
const meter = @import("meter.rye");

pub const stereo_crop_version = "20260815.015904";

/// stereo_crop forwards ALES2's OWN fault reused by name — BadRange (a kept span outside the samples) — and invents none of its own.
pub const StereoCropError = timeline.EditError;

/// Keep [start, start+count) in both channels and drop every sample before and after, in lockstep — the stereo image staying balanced, each channel's length becoming exactly count.
/// Both channels are validated before either is mutated: because mono crop can fault only with BadRange and the channels enter equal-length, the shared span is checked once against the
/// common length up front, so a refusal never leaves one channel cropped and the other not. Reuses ALES111's mono crop per channel.
pub fn stereo_crop(sc: *pan.StereoClip, start: u32, count: u32) StereoCropError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: the kept span lies wholly within the (shared) current samples, checked without underflow, refused by name before any write — the same guard ALES111's crop keeps, run
    //            once against the common length so a refusal lands before either channel mutates a single sample and the channels can never desynchronise.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels, so each mono crop is pre-validated to succeed — no refusal can land between the two channel edits.
    try crop.crop(&sc.left, start, count);
    try crop.crop(&sc.right, start, count);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced and are each exactly the kept span — the stereo image stays aligned.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    assert(sc.left.len == count);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO CROP LAW — each channel equals ALES111's mono crop on that channel byte for byte; THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW — left.len == right.len == count after; THE
//     COMPLEMENT LAW — stereo_crop keeps what ALES117's stereo_cut removes per channel; THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW — a refusal leaves both channels untouched, the identity and
//     empty faces per channel ----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO CROP LAW — each channel equals ALES111's mono crop on that channel, proven side by side over one anchor with different per-channel samples.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 }, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        try stereo_crop(&sc, 1, 3); // keep samples 1,2,3 in both

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 });
        try crop.crop(&ml, 1, 3);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        try crop.crop(&mr, 1, 3);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // Concretely: the kept middle span, on each channel.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 20, 30, 40 })) return error.LeftSpanWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 21, 31, 41 })) return error.RightSpanWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW — the channels stay equal length and each becomes exactly count across a mid span, the head, and the tail; the stereo image stays aligned.
    {
        var mid = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&mid, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 }, &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 });
        try stereo_crop(&mid, 1, 3);
        if (mid.left.len != mid.right.len) return error.MidUnbalanced;
        if (mid.left.len != 3) return error.MidLength;

        var head = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&head, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4 }, &[_]i16{ 5, 6, 7, 8 });
        try stereo_crop(&head, 0, 2);
        if (head.left.len != head.right.len) return error.HeadUnbalanced;
        if (!eq(head.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2 })) return error.HeadLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(head.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 5, 6 })) return error.HeadRightWrong;

        var tail = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&tail, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4 }, &[_]i16{ 5, 6, 7, 8 });
        try stereo_crop(&tail, 2, 2); // keep through the tail
        if (tail.left.len != tail.right.len) return error.TailUnbalanced;
        if (!eq(tail.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 3, 4 })) return error.TailLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(tail.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 8 })) return error.TailRightWrong;
    }

    // 3. THE COMPLEMENT LAW — stereo_crop keeps exactly what ALES117's stereo_cut removes, per channel: crop(1, 2) yields the middle span; cut(1, 2) yields the head-plus-tail around
    //    it; splicing the crop back at 1 into the cut reconstructs the source on each channel, so crop and cut partition the span in stereo too.
    {
        const srcl = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };
        const srcr = [_]i16{ 50, 60, 70, 80 };

        var kept = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&kept, &srcl, &srcr);
        try stereo_crop(&kept, 1, 2);
        if (!eq(kept.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 20, 30 })) return error.CropMiddleLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(kept.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 60, 70 })) return error.CropMiddleRightWrong;

        var removed = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&removed, &srcl, &srcr);
        // stereo_cut is the same gesture run per channel; use timeline.cut per channel here to keep this module standing only on ALES111 + ALES2.
        try timeline.cut(&removed.left, 1, 2);
        try timeline.cut(&removed.right, 1, 2);
        if (!eq(removed.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 40 })) return error.CutAroundLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(removed.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 50, 80 })) return error.CutAroundRightWrong;

        // Splice the kept middle back at 1 into the cut's head-plus-tail on each channel — the source returns byte for byte, proving crop and cut partition the span in stereo.
        var rebuilt = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&rebuilt, removed.left.samples(), removed.right.samples());
        try timeline.splice(&rebuilt.left, 1, kept.left.samples());
        try timeline.splice(&rebuilt.right, 1, kept.right.samples());
        if (!eq(rebuilt.left.samples(), &srcl)) return error.PartitionLeftBroken;
        if (!eq(rebuilt.right.samples(), &srcr)) return error.PartitionRightBroken;
    }

    // 4a. THE ATOMICITY LAW — a refusal leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced, proven for a start past the samples and a count past the remainder.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 });

        if (stereo_crop(&sc, 4, 1)) |_| return error.StartNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        if (stereo_crop(&sc, 2, 5)) |_| return error.CountNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.RefusedWroteLeft;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 })) return error.RefusedWroteRight;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.RefusedUnbalanced;
    }

    // 4b. THE DEGENERATE LAW — crop(0, len) the identity on both channels, crop(start, 0) the empty pair (both length 0, still balanced).
    {
        var idc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&idc, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 });
        try stereo_crop(&idc, 0, idc.left.len); // keep the whole clip — the identity
        if (!eq(idc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 })) return error.WholeCropLeftMoved;
        if (!eq(idc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.WholeCropRightMoved;

        var emptied = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&emptied, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 });
        try stereo_crop(&emptied, 1, 0); // zero count — the empty pair
        if (emptied.left.len != 0 or emptied.right.len != 0) return error.ZeroCountNotEmpty;
        if (emptied.left.len != emptied.right.len) return error.EmptyUnbalanced;
    }

    _ = meter;

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-crop: STEREO_CROP — Trim to Selection carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. Keep [start, start+count) in both channels and drop the rest, " ++
        "reusing ALES111's mono crop per channel; a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10) whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length, so circling the one " ++
        "phrase worth keeping trims both speakers to it at once. Where ALES117's stereo_cut removes the span and keeps everything around it, stereo_crop keeps only the span — the " ++
        "exact stereo complement. BOTH channels are validated before either is mutated — because mono crop can fault only with BadRange and the channels enter equal-length, the " ++
        "shared span is checked once against the common length up front, so a refusal never desynchronises the channels. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO CROP LAW: each channel " ++
        "equals ALES111's mono crop on that channel byte for byte. THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW: left.len == right.len == count after the edit, the stereo image staying aligned. THE " ++
        "COMPLEMENT LAW: stereo_crop keeps exactly what stereo_cut removes per channel, the crop spliced back into the cut reconstructing the source on each channel. THE ATOMICITY / " ++
        "DEGENERATE LAW: any refusal leaves both channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced, crop(0, len) the identity on both, crop(start, 0) the empty pair, a span past " ++
        "the samples BadRange. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips reseated through the proven mono crop, reading no byte past either channel's len; no real sample rate, no network, " ++
        "no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
